WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.220
How do you write a

2
00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:03.220
proof that actually proves the

3
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.500
claim? A proof must explain

4
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.660
why every permitted case satisfies

5
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.660
the statement, rather than display

6
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.960
a few attractive examples. We

7
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.860
will connect two-way set reasoning,

8
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.200
induction, and the pigeonhole principle

9
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.120
to automata.

10
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.160
Suppose we want to prove

11
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.640
that two sets are equal.

12
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.480
Showing that every member of

13
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.680
the first belongs to the

14
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.560
second proves one inclusion only.

15
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.440
We must also start with

16
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.860
an arbitrary member of the

17
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.540
second set and establish the

18
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.460
reverse inclusion.

19
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.460
For example, consider the complement

20
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.060
of a union. An object

21
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.820
is outside the union exactly

22
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.500
when it is outside both

23
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.480
constituent sets. Expanding membership conditions

24
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.500
in both directions proves the

25
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.560
De Morgan identity, not just

26
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.140
its picture.

27
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.120
An if and only if

28
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.840
statement has the same two-way

29
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.180
obligation. If you prove that

30
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.180
acceptance implies the desired language

31
00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.940
property, you have ruled out

32
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.320
extra accepted words. You must

33
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.220
still prove that every desired

34
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.980
word is accepted, or your

35
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.180
machine could reject everything and

36
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.860
satisfy the first implication.

37
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.680
For induction, first identify the

38
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.380
quantity that becomes smaller. In

39
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.120
automata, it is often the

40
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.960
length of the already-read prefix.

41
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.680
State the invariant precisely before

42
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.520
writing the base case, so

43
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.980
the induction hypothesis is something

44
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.340
you can actually use.

45
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.260
Take a machine whose state

46
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.880
records whether the latest symbol

47
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.020
is one. Initially the prefix

48
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.620
is empty, so the special

49
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.200
start condition must agree with

50
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.880
that invariant. If the next

51
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.580
symbol is one, enter the

52
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.280
one-state; if it is zero,

53
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.380
enter the other state, preserving

54
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.840
the claim after one additional

55
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.300
symbol.

56
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.080
The induction hypothesis does not

57
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.580
assume the final result for

58
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.340
every word. It assumes the

59
00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.720
stated property for a shorter

60
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.580
prefix, then derives it for

61
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.820
the next prefix. Your diagram

62
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.700
can guide that step, but

63
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.260
a collection of tested inputs

64
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.360
cannot replace it.

65
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.800
The pigeonhole principle supplies a

66
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.680
different kind of proof. If

67
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:04.860
a run visits more state

68
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.160
positions than the machine has

69
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.420
states, two positions must contain

70
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.500
the same state. In particular,

71
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.080
the first p consumed symbols

72
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.640
give p plus one visited

73
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.200
states when the starting position

74
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:15.880
is included.

75
00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.480
Finally, inspect who chooses each

76
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.700
object in the statement. Proving

77
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.640
that one decomposition fails is

78
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.300
not proving that every decomposition

79
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.320
fails. When a theorem says

80
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.060
there exists a successful split,

81
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.520
a contradiction must eliminate every

82
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.120
split that obeys its conditions.

83
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.900
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.560
For equality and equivalence, write

85
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.240
both directions; for induction, write

86
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.140
the base, hypothesis and preservation

87
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.120
step. For impossibility proofs, make

88
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.560
the arbitrary choices explicit, and

89
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.100
never let a true conclusion

90
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.820
excuse an invalid argument.
